  "articleBody": "It’s easy to make a few slip-ups when tackling kitchen cabinet removal, but knowing what to look out for can save you a lot of trouble. One common mistake is forgetting to shut off utilities. Seriously, always turn off the water and power to your kitchen before you start! You don't want a surprise flood or an electrical shock. Another big one is not cutting the caulk line. If you try to pry cabinets off the wall without cutting the caulk first, you’re almost guaranteed to damage the drywall, which means more repair work later. Also, when you're taking down upper cabinets, don't just unscrew them and let them drop. Make sure they're supported or have a second person to help you, or you could end up with a broken cabinet or even an injury. And don't forget those sneaky plumbing or electrical connections that might be attached to the cabinets! Always double-check for them before you start pulling anything away. If these sound like too many potential pitfalls, or you're dealing with complex setups, bringing in a pro like HH Remodeling LLC can really take the stress out of the process and ensure everything is handled correctly.",
